WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.600
How do you design a

2
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:03.840
deterministic automaton from the memory

3
00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:06.460
it needs? Consider binary strings

4
00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:08.520
containing zero zero one as

5
00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.440
a substring. Instead of guessing

6
00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:12.820
a picture, identify which useful

7
00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:14.520
prefixes of that pattern have

8
00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:15.620
already been matched.

9
00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:17.540
Before a match is complete,

10
00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:19.520
remember the longest suffix that

11
00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:21.100
is also a prefix of

12
00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:23.360
zero zero one. The possibilities

13
00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:25.180
are no useful suffix, a

14
00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:27.180
single zero, and two zeros.

15
00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:28.660
Add a fourth state for

16
00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:30.540
a pattern already found, because

17
00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:32.620
future input cannot undo a

18
00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:33.560
contained substring.

19
00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:35.960
From no useful suffix, reading

20
00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:38.000
zero starts a partial match.

21
00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:40.280
Reading one leaves no useful

22
00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:41.800
suffix, so stay in the

23
00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:44.320
start state. These two transitions

24
00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:46.380
cover both possible next symbols

25
00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:48.760
without requiring any unbounded memory.

26
00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:51.700
From the single-zero state, another

27
00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:53.620
zero extends the remembered suffix

28
00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:55.040
to two zeros. A one

29
00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:56.800
would produce zero one, which

30
00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:58.600
has no nonempty suffix matching

31
00:00:58.600 --> 00:00:59.560
the start of our pattern.

32
00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.480
Therefore that one-transition returns to

33
00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:03.960
the no-useful-suffix state.

34
00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:06.480
From two zeros, reading another

35
00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:08.460
zero must keep the two-zero

36
00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:10.520
state. The full prefix may

37
00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:12.300
have three zeros, but its

38
00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:13.920
last two zeros remain a

39
00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:16.620
useful candidate. Resetting here would

40
00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:18.500
lose the overlapping match in

41
00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:20.040
zero zero zero one.

42
00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:22.020
Now read one from that

43
00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:24.840
two-zero state. The remembered zeros

44
00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:26.580
followed by this one complete

45
00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:28.560
the target substring, so enter

46
00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:30.620
the accepting state. Both later

47
00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:32.200
symbols loop there because the

48
00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:33.920
occurrence we already found remains

49
00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:35.460
inside every extension.

50
00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:37.580
The proof follows the state

51
00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:39.000
meanings rather than a list

52
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.120
of successful test words. The

53
00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:43.040
start state correctly represents the

54
00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:45.300
empty prefix, and each transition

55
00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.320
updates the longest useful suffix

56
00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:49.200
or preserves a completed match.

57
00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:51.240
Thus the accepting state is

58
00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:53.440
reached exactly for prefixes that

59
00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:54.460
contain the pattern.

60
00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:57.180
Other languages require different finite

61
00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:59.720
memories. Odd parity needs only

62
00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:01.800
even and odd states, while

63
00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:03.340
equality of zero and one

64
00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:05.240
counts modulo three needs the

65
00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:08.060
count difference modulo three. Exact

66
00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:10.260
unbounded equality cannot be replaced

67
00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:12.100
by that modular test, because

68
00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:13.720
counts differing by three would

69
00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:14.340
pass it.

70
00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:16.300
Change the task to strings

71
00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:17.980
ending in zero zero one.

72
00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:19.760
The pattern state can no

73
00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:22.140
longer be permanently accepting, because

74
00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:23.680
another symbol can change the

75
00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:25.940
suffix. A small wording change

76
00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:27.440
in the language therefore changes

77
00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:29.360
the memory update, even when

78
00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:30.620
the target pattern is the

79
00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:31.000
same.

80
00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:32.900
Things to take note of.

81
00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.500
Define state meanings first, write

82
00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:37.760
every alphabet transition, and use

83
00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:39.580
the longest relevant suffix to

84
00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:42.000
handle overlaps. Then test the

85
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:43.520
empty word and near misses,

86
00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:45.420
and give an invariant explaining

87
00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:47.360
why the machine accepts exactly

88
00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:48.500
the required language.
